WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:03.590
Narrator: This is the map
of a typical human brain,

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:07.350
and this is the map of
a brain on psilocybin,

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.873
the psychoactive compound
in magic mushrooms.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.590
All those new connections you can see

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:14.690
don't just make people trip.

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:16.920
They're also the reason that psilocybin

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.330
is one of today's most talked-about drugs

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:20.803
in certain medical circles.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.100
Worldwide, more than
180 species of mushrooms

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:32.820
produce psilocybin, likely
as a defense strategy.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:34.530
Scientists believe that psilocybin

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:38.140
may dampen the appetite of
predatory insects like ants

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:40.790
so that they feel full long
before eating their way

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:42.910
through the entire mushroom.

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:46.570
Humans, on the other
hand, well, they trip.

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:50.660
Johnson: Psilocybin is a
so-called classic psychedelic,

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:54.710
so it's in the same
category as drugs like LSD

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:57.380
and works in the brain in
basically the same way.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:00:59.860
Narrator: When you take
psilocybin, your gut converts it

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:02.370
into another chemical, known as psilocin,

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:06.010
which binds to serotonin
receptors called 2A,

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:08.170
and experts think that's what triggers

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:11.000
what they call neuronal avalanching.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.590
It's essentially a domino effect

00:01:12.590 --> 00:01:14.480
of different changes in the brain.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:16.910
You've got increased activity
in the visual cortex,

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:19.070
which leads to changes in your perception,

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:21.020
and then decreased network activity

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:24.380
in the default mode network,
which leads to a loss of ego.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:27.747
Johnson: And that may be
why people often report

00:01:27.747 --> 00:01:29.213
at high doses

00:01:29.213 --> 00:01:33.600
a profound sense of unity,
transcending beyond themselves.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.530
Narrator: But perhaps most importantly,

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:37.660
psilocybin increases connectivity

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:39.400
among different regions of the brain.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.890
Johnson: Because of that
receptor activation,

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:44.680
there is a profound change

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:48.410
in the way that different
areas of the brain

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:50.340
synchronize with each other.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:52.240
Narrator: Think of it like an orchestra.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:54.810
Normally, the brain has
different musical groups

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.400
that each play independently.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.300
Johnson: A sextet there,
here's a quartet there.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.830
This one's playing jazz.
This one's classical,

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:03.990
and a number of other ones.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:05.860
Narrator: But once psilocybin enters,

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:07.820
it's like you suddenly have a conductor.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:10.960
Johnson: So there is this
communication between areas

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:13.770
that are normally kind
of compartmentalized

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.090
and doing their own thing.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:16.780
Narrator: Scientists believe
that it's a combination

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:19.610
of these effects that
make psilocybin so useful

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:21.633
for combating depression and addiction.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:24.850
When new areas in the brain
start talking to each other,

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:28.830
for example, you might have
new insights into old problems.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:31.090
And that's why some
experts describe tripping

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:33.600
as a condensed version of talk therapy.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.830
And then dissolving your
ego, Johnson says...

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:38.850
Johnson: Can be profoundly healing.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:40.290
Narrator: And there's
actually an increasing amount

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.140
of research to prove it.

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:44.290
In two studies published in 2016,

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:46.670
researchers gave cancer
patients with depression

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:50.260
a large dose of psilocybin,
and even six months later,

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:53.300
at least 80% of them showed
significant decreases

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.140
in depressed mood.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:58.070
And research on addiction
is equally promising.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:01.470
In a study led by Johnson,
15 volunteers took psilocybin

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:04.280
to quit smoking, and after six months,

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:06.363
80% of them had kicked the habit,

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:09.720
compared to a rate of about 35%

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:12.440
for the drug varenicline,
which is widely considered

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:15.510
the best smoking-cessation drug out there.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.400
Yet despite these results,

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.800
psilocybin is still listed
as a Schedule I drug,

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.610
a category reserved for compounds

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:24.990
that have no currently
accepted medical use

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:27.400
and a high potential for abuse.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.840
Now, taking magic mushrooms recreationally

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:31.400
does come with some risks.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:34.340
Johnson: So a dramatic example would be

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:37.590
driving under the influence of psilocybin

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:42.210
or using it in a way that
interferes with your job,

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:46.030
or your family relations, or
your schoolwork, for example.

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:47.740
Narrator: But as far as scientists know,

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:49.670
long-term use doesn't damage the brain

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:51.620
in the way that other drugs can,

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:53.470
and according to at least one study,

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:55.940
it's actually the safest drug out there.

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:59.220
In 2018, for example, just 0.3% of people

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:02.600
who reported taking them needed
medical emergency treatment,

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:07.600
compared to 0.9% for ecstasy
and 1.3% for alcohol.

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:10.850
Taken altogether, that's why some states

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:12.400
across the country have campaigned

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.110
to decriminalize psilocybin,
including Denver,

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:20.163
which, in May of 2019, became
the first ever to succeed.

